.TH LLAVERO 1 "September 2026" "llavero" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
llavero \- software FIDO2 authenticator for Linux
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B llavero
[\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B llavero
is a software FIDO2 authenticator.
It registers a virtual FIDO HID device with the kernel so browsers discover
it the same way they discover a hardware security key: no extension, no
native messaging host, no browser configuration.
.PP
Passkeys live in a single AES-256-GCM encrypted vault, optionally sealed to
this machine's TPM.
Every registration and every sign-in requires user verification
(a fingerprint by default) and an explicit desktop approval that names the
site.
.PP
With no action flags,
.B llavero
unlocks the vault and runs as the authenticator daemon.
.BR \-list ,
.BR \-forget ,
.B \-rekey
and
.B \-tpm-selftest
run their action and exit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-vault " PATH"
Path to the encrypted vault file.
Default:
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/llavero/vault.pkv
or
.I \%~/.local/share/llavero/vault.pkv
if
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
is unset.
A TPM-bound vault also keeps a sealed blob at
.IR PATH.tpm .
.TP
.BI \-unlock " MODE"
Unlock mode used only when creating a
.B new
vault:
.BR passphrase ,
.BR tpm ,
or
.BR tpm+passphrase .
The mode is recorded in the vault header, so later starts ask only for what
that vault actually needs.
Default:
.BR passphrase .
.TP
.BI \-rekey " MODE"
Re-encrypt an existing vault under
.I MODE
and exit.
The original file is copied to a timestamped
.B .bak-*
backup first, and success is reported only after the rewritten vault reopens
with the same credential count.
.TP
.BI \-uv " METHOD"
User verification:
.B fingerprint
(the default) or
.BR prompt
(the desktop approval alone).
.TP
.B \-uv-strict
Deny the request when the fingerprint sensor is unusable, instead of falling
back to the desktop approval.
The packaged user unit sets this.
It is an example of failing closed on a vault that unlocks itself at login,
not a setting tuned to one machine.
.TP
.BI \-consent " METHOD"
How to take consent:
.B prompt
(click to approve, then touch; the default) or
.B fingerprint
(touch only).
.B \-consent fingerprint
falls back to the approval prompt if no sensor is available, so a page
cannot mint passkeys in silence.
.TP
.BI \-uv-grace " DURATION"
Reuse a just-completed fingerprint scan for repeat requests from the
.B same
site.
Clients often fire two
.B getAssertion
calls milliseconds apart for one sign-in.
Default: 5s.
.B 0
disables reuse.
.TP
.BI \-mlock
Lock process memory so decrypted keys cannot be written to swap.
Default: on.
Pass
.B \-mlock=false
to turn it off.
.I mlockall
needs the
.B RLIMIT_MEMLOCK
ceiling raised on the
.B systemd --user
manager; see
.I packaging/README.md
and the
.B user@.service.d
drop-in.
.TP
.B \-list
Print stored passkeys and exit.
.TP
.BI \-forget " SITE|PREFIX"
Delete passkeys matching a relying-party ID or a credential-id prefix, then
exit.
Shows what it will delete and asks you to type the same string back.
Refuses to run while
.B llavero.service
has that same vault open, because the daemon holds its own copy in memory
and its next write would resurrect anything deleted underneath it.
.TP
.BI \-passphrase-fd " N"
Read the vault passphrase from file descriptor
.IR N
.RB ( 0
for stdin).
.TP
.BI \-new-passphrase-fd " N"
Read the new passphrase for
.B \-rekey
from file descriptor
.IR N .
.TP
.B \-tpm-selftest
Seal and unseal a test secret, confirm two seals differ, confirm a
corrupted blob is rejected, then exit.
.TP
.B \-auto-approve
Approve every request without prompting.
Testing only.
.TP
.B \-v
Log every CTAPHID frame.
.TP
.B \-h
Print flag defaults and exit.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/llavero/vault.pkv
Default vault.
The matching TPM blob, if any, is
.IR vault.pkv.tpm .
.TP
.I /dev/uhid
Kernel device used to register the virtual HID authenticator.
The packaged udev rule grants the active seat access; without it the daemon
exits with
.BR "no access to /dev/uhid" .
.TP
.I /dev/tpmrm0
TPM 2.0 resource manager, required for
.B tpm
and
.B tpm+passphrase
unlock.
The packaged
.B tpmrm
udev rule is preferred over joining the
.B tss
group: supplementary groups are fixed when a process starts, and the
.B systemd --user
manager commonly survives logout.
.TP
.I ~/.local/bin/llavero
Where a from-source install should place the binary.
The user unit's
.B ExecStart
is
.IR %h/.local/bin/llavero .
.B go install
writes to
.B GOBIN
(or
.IR $(go\ env\ GOPATH)/bin );
set
.B GOBIN=~/.local/bin
so the unit finds it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a completed daemon shutdown after SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.TP
.B 1
The requested action failed (permissions, a locked vault, a refused
.BR \-forget ,
and so on).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
Directory under which the default vault is stored
.RI ( .../llavero/vault.pkv ).
.TP
.BR WAYLAND_DISPLAY ", " DISPLAY
At least one must be set for the desktop approval prompt.
Without a display every request would be refused with no visible reason.
.SH EXAMPLES
Create a TPM-unlocked vault and start the user service:
.PP
.nf
.RS
llavero \-unlock tpm
systemctl \-\-user enable \-\-now llavero.service
.RE
.fi
.PP
List stored passkeys:
.PP
.nf
.RS
llavero \-list
.RE
.fi
.PP
Delete every passkey for a site (stop the service first):
.PP
.nf
.RS
systemctl \-\-user stop llavero.service
llavero \-forget dash.example.com
.RE
.fi
.PP
Rekey a
.B copy
of a TPM-bound vault to a portable passphrase backup.
Both the vault and its
.B .tpm
blob must be copied; the live vault is left untouched:
.PP
.nf
.RS
cp ~/.local/share/llavero/vault.pkv     /tmp/portable.pkv
cp ~/.local/share/llavero/vault.pkv.tpm /tmp/portable.pkv.tpm
llavero \-vault /tmp/portable.pkv \-rekey passphrase
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR systemd (1),
.BR udev (7),
.BR fprintd (1)
.PP
The repository README covers the security model, browser behaviour, and
setup in more detail.
Packaging files (udev rules, the user unit, this page, and shell
completions) live in
.IR packaging/ .
.SH NOTES
.B llavero
does not implement CTAP 2.1 credential management or PIN protocols.
.B CTAPHID_CANCEL
is not honoured while an approval dialog is on screen; a cancelled
WebAuthn request times out rather than aborting immediately.
.PP
Chrome on Linux may register a passkey into its own profile store instead
of this vault.
Prefer Firefox for registrations you intend to keep, and look for a
.B registered passkey for
line in the journal.
